WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.837
Sampling: why the same prompt gives different answers.

2
00:00:03.117 --> 00:00:08.941
At each step the model produces a score, called a logit, for every token in the vocabulary.

3
00:00:09.221 --> 00:00:12.613
The softmax function turns those scores into probabilities.

4
00:00:12.893 --> 00:00:14.792
Then something has to pick a token.

5
00:00:15.072 --> 00:00:22.411
Always taking the most likely token (greedy decoding) sounds safe, but it tends to produce repetitive, flat text.

6
00:00:22.691 --> 00:00:28.429
Sampling from the distribution produces more natural text, at the cost of run-to-run variation.

7
00:00:28.709 --> 00:00:30.885
Two classic controls shape that sampling.

8
00:00:31.165 --> 00:00:40.189
Temperature divides every logit by a number before the softmax: below 1 sharpens the distribution toward the top choices, above 1 flattens it.

9
00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:54.699
Top-p (nucleus sampling, introduced by Holtzman and colleagues in 2019) samples only from the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to p, which cuts off the long tail of unlikely tokens.

10
00:00:54.979 --> 00:01:02.445
If you have a math background, temperature is a familiar object: it is the same parameter as in the Boltzmann distribution.

11
00:01:02.725 --> 00:01:07.397
Probability of token i is proportional to e raised to the logit over T.

12
00:01:07.677 --> 00:01:12.883
As T approaches zero you recover the argmax; as T grows you approach uniform.
